-- title:
--   Theorem 1 — the trajectory-based algorithm drives the truncated and untruncated cost gradients to zero almost surely
-- statement:
--   Let Assumptions 1 to 3 and 5 hold. Let $\{\theta_m\}$ be the sequence generated by Eq. (11) (or Eq. (12)).
--
--   (i) Suppose that $\{M_m\}_{m\ge0}$ is a fixed sequence, that is, $M_m=M$ for all $m\ge0$, with $M>1$. Then $\Lambda^{(M)}_{\theta_m}$ converges and
--   $$\nabla_\theta\Lambda^{(M)}_{\theta_m}\to0\quad\text{with probability }1.$$
--
--   (ii) Suppose that $\sum_m\gamma_m^{2(1-\beta)}<\infty$ for some fixed $\beta\in(0,0.5)$, and the truncation sequence is given by $M_m:=\sup\{N_i:N_i\le1/\gamma_m^\beta\}$, where $\{N_i\}_{i\ge0}$ is a sequence for which Assumption 4 holds. Then $\Lambda_{\theta_m}$ converges and
--   $$\nabla_\theta\Lambda_{\theta_m}\to0\quad\text{with probability }1.$$
--
--   This is the main result of the paper. A policy updated once per cycle from a single trajectory reaches a stationary point of the risk-sensitive cost, although the natural gradient statistic can have infinite variance.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The statement holds for every run of the algorithm on every probability space, for each update rule. Differentiability of the cost and infinitely many returns to $x^*$ are added conclusions, which exclude junk values of `gradient` and of $\theta_m$. No monotonicity of $\gamma_m$ or $M_m$ is assumed in (ii), as on the page; see the mission's notes on the proof for that case.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: M_m := sup{N_i : N_i ≤ 1/γ_m^β}, undefined when that set is empty → M_m is any value above 1 where the set is empty; reason: the set is empty for at most finitely many m, and every way of filling that case is covered
--   - reading: t₀ = 0 with t_m the m-th visit to x*, and a step-size sequence γ_m → Φ₀ = x* almost surely, and γ_m > 0; reason: Step 0 (p.24) takes Φ_{t_m} = x* for every m including 0; Theorem 1(ii) writes 1/γ_m^β
--   - encoding: the adaptive chain and the sequence θ_m → IsRun gives the law of each step given the whole prefix, and θ_m is the fold of the update at the m-th return; reason: the chain is not the Markov chain of one θ; the fold is the recursion on the observed cycles
--   - reading: bounded functions and bounded derivatives in Assumptions 2 and 3 → bounds uniform in θ, with no continuity of the second derivative; reason: Lemma 12's proof and Lemma 17 need uniform bounds; the page says twice differentiable, not C²
--   - reading: M with no lower bound in the statement → M > 1; reason: §5.2, p.8 defines g^(M), f^(M) and Λ^(M) only for M > 1
--   - encoding: irreducible and aperiodic → Matrix.IsPrimitive; reason: for a stochastic matrix on a finite type these are the same condition; no instance changes
--   - encoding: expectations over the first return time τ_{x*} → sums over finite excursions, in ℝ≥0∞ or as a real sum with its summability asserted; reason: Assumption 1 makes τ_{x*} finite almost surely, so the values agree and +∞ stays representable
--   - encoding: Assumption 4 with a supremum over θ → Assumption 4 with a summable majorant d; reason: the differences are nonnegative by Corollary 14, so the conditions are equivalent, and no real supremum of an unbounded family arises
--   - reading: N_i ↑ ∞ in Assumption 4 → N monotone (nondecreasing), tending to ∞, with N_i > 1 for every i; reason: ↑ ∞ means nondecreasing to ∞; §5.2 (p.8) defines Λ^(M) only for M > 1, so the terms Λ^(N_i) of Assumption 4 presuppose N_i > 1
--   - encoding: Λ^(M)_θm converges and ∇_θ Λ^(M)_θm → 0 with probability 1, and the same for Λ → differentiability of the cost, and x* visited infinitely often almost surely, as added conclusions; reason: the added clauses strengthen the theorem and exclude junk values of gradient and of θ_m
-- source:
--   Moharrami, Murthy, Roy & Srikant, A Policy Gradient Algorithm for the Risk-Sensitive Exponential Cost MDP, arXiv:2202.04157v2, Theorem 1, p.11

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RiskExpPG_Chain
import Definitions.Def_RiskExpPG_Model
import Definitions.Def_RiskExpPG_Algorithm
open Filter Topology MeasureTheory
open scoped ENNReal

namespace RiskExpPG

/-- **Theorem 1** (arXiv:2202.04157v2, p.11). Let Assumptions 1 to 3 and 5 hold, and let `{θ_k}` be
generated by Eq. (11) or Eq. (12), along any run of the algorithm.

(i) If `M_k = M` for all `k`, with `M > 1`, then `Λ^(M)` is differentiable and, with probability `1`,
`x*` is visited infinitely often, `Λ^(M)_{θ_k}` converges and `∇_θ Λ^(M)_{θ_k} → 0`.

(ii) If `Σ_k γ_k^{2(1-β)} < ∞` for some fixed `β ∈ (0, 0.5)` and `M_k := sup{N_i : N_i ≤ 1/γ_k^β}`, where
`{N_i}` is a sequence for which Assumption 4 holds, then `Λ` is differentiable and, with
probability `1`, `x*` is visited infinitely often, `Λ_{θ_k}` converges and `∇_θ Λ_{θ_k} → 0`.

**Formalization Note** The differentiability of the cost and the infinitely many returns are added
conclusions. They exclude the junk value `gradient f = 0` of a non-differentiable `f`, and the
junk value of `θ_k` on a trajectory with finitely many returns. -/
theorem theorem_1 {X : Type*} [Fintype X] [DecidableEq X] {l : ℕ} (m : Model X l)
    (hm : m.IsValid) (h1 : m.Assumption1) (h2 : m.Assumption2) (h3 : m.Assumption3)
    (cfg : Config l) (hcfg : cfg.IsValid)
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) (Φ : ℕ → Ω → X) (hrun : IsRun m cfg μ Φ) :
    (∀ M, cfg.FixedTruncation M →
      Differentiable ℝ (m.truncCost M) ∧
      ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ,
        (∀ n₀, ∃ n ≥ n₀, Φ n ω = m.xstar) ∧
        (∃ c, Tendsto (fun k => m.truncCost M (m.thetaAt cfg (fun i => Φ i ω) k)) atTop (𝓝 c)) ∧
        Tendsto (fun k => gradient (m.truncCost M) (m.thetaAt cfg (fun i => Φ i ω) k))
          atTop (𝓝 0)) ∧
    (∀ β N, cfg.GrowingTruncation m β N →
      Differentiable ℝ m.riskCost ∧
      ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ,
        (∀ n₀, ∃ n ≥ n₀, Φ n ω = m.xstar) ∧
        (∃ c, Tendsto (fun k => m.riskCost (m.thetaAt cfg (fun i => Φ i ω) k)) atTop (𝓝 c)) ∧
        Tendsto (fun k => gradient m.riskCost (m.thetaAt cfg (fun i => Φ i ω) k))
          atTop (𝓝 0)) := by sorry

end RiskExpPG
